  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14187244/assets/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=25$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=21$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=15$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*",
